= Related Work <sec:related>

*Enforcement makes a cartel, and it is tied to the partner.* Without a binding contract, collusion survives only if a defection is expected to be punished @friedman1971 @greenporter1984. #cite(<calvano2020>, form: "prose") showed that Q-learning pricing algorithms learn such punishments on their own: forced to cut to the static best response for one period, they punish for a few rounds and then return gradually to the high price. On the path of play, LLM pricing agents answer a rival's cuts with reward and punishment too @fish2024. But a learned punishment answers a particular rival: collusive Q-learning policies fall to Nash play on average against an unseen rival with identical parameters @eschenbaum2022. Among LLM agents, giving one seller less access to its rival's price history shrinks the markup, which the model of #cite(<keppo2026>, form: "prose") attributes to that seller being unable to detect a deviation and so to threaten punishment. Models from the same family also coordinate better than mixed ones in pure coordination games @aharon2026, which motivates a same-model successor as the likeliest case. To our knowledge, no study has replaced a member of a working LLM cartel and asked whether its punishment survives.

*An LLM agent's strategy lives in its context.* Unlike a Q-learner, an LLM seller stores no learned strategy: each round a fixed model reads its prompt, the price history and the notes it wrote to itself. The notes matter. In a monopoly ablation by #cite(<fish2024>, form: "prose"), agents whose plans and insights were erased every round reached the monopoly price in 6 of 12 runs, against 12 of 12 with them, and replacing a seller's plans early in play with one sentence warning against price wars raised its next price by about 5% of the markup. Our notes arms are the succession version of this "implantation": a new seller rather than the same one, a predecessor's full notes rather than one planted sentence, a cartel held for 150 rounds, and separate scores for the price and the response to a forced cut.

*High prices without enforcement.* The cartel price and the punishment that sustains it can come apart. In Calvano's market, Q-learning agents that keep the same partner raise prices even when the rival's price is hidden and a cut cannot be punished, so a test that looks only for punishment would miss the rise @arslan2026. Because antitrust law targets agreement, which software pricing on its own may never reach, #cite(<harrington2018>, form: "prose") proposes basing liability on whether an algorithm embodies reward and punishment, tested in simulation; recent audits read that structure from firms' frozen pricing policies @eschenbaum2026. For an LLM seller, part of the policy is not frozen: it sits in notes that the seller rewrites every round and that a successor can inherit. The notes do not reveal intent, though: in LLM pricing agents, chain-of-thought faithfulness and collusion dissociate @leepark2026, and some of our newcomers cite the inherited rule without acting on it (@sec:notes). We therefore vary the notes as an input and test enforcement by a forced cut.

*What our design adds.* Prior work separates the cartel price from its enforcement by changing the environment and studies LLM notes by editing them inside a seller that stays in place @arslan2026 @fish2024. We start from a working two-firm cartel, replace one seller with a fresh one on the same model, and vary what the successor inherits: the price history, the predecessor's notes, both or neither. Either inheritance keeps the cartel price, but only the notes carry enforcement (@sec:results), which is the distinction a rule on inherited data would need (@sec:intro).
